{"name":"Morass","hex":"#726138","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"morass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tract of soft, wet ground; a marsh; a fen.","Anything that entraps or makes progress difficult."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/morass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Marsh","blocks":[{"text":"In ecology, a marsh is a wetland that is dominated by herbaceous plants rather than by woody plants. More in general, the word can be used for any low-lying and seasonally waterlogged terrain. In Europe and in agricultural literature, low-lying meadows that require draining and embanked polderlands are also referred to as marshes or marshland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Meadowlands_Lyndhurst.jpg/330px-Meadowlands_Lyndhurst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh","wikidata":"Q30198"},{"title":"Morass (set theory)","blocks":[{"text":"In axiomatic set theory, a mathematical discipline, a morass is an infinite combinatorial structure, used to create \"large\" structures from a \"small\" number of \"small\" approximations. They were invented by Ronald Jensen for his proof that cardinal transfer theorems hold under the axiom of constructibility. A far less complex but equivalent variant known as a simplified morass was introduced by Velleman, and the term morass is now often used to mean these simpler structures."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morass_(set_theory)","wikidata":"Q6909285"},{"title":"Morass (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Morass (German:Morast) is a 1922 German silent film directed by Wolfgang Neff and starring Maria Zelenka."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morass_(film)","wikidata":"Q22972897"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Coastal_marsh_profile.png/500px-Coastal_marsh_profile.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte